From d2e1825a9fe0208e8585e28adda4a368f1e80ac8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 05:39:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: a plain picture drawn through an upscaler's
 mpv shaders, and an SDR one as it is

The prescalers of mpv (RAVU, FSRCNNX) work on the luma plane of a
picture as it is coded, before its chroma is scaled and before it is
converted to RGB. Kodi's GLES renderer cannot host them: it converts
and scales in one shader pass into RGB. libplacebo's renderer can,
through pl_mpv_user_shader_parse(), and it is the renderer that the
bridge already draws plain pictures with.

dvbridge_upscale loads an upscaler's shader files (a fixed list of
names, each under 2 MB, read from the folder the caller gives) and
dvbridge_render_set_upscaler() hands them to the following pictures of
dvbridge_render_plain(), after Hades' own hooks, for a plain picture
that is not sent over the tunnel. libplacebo decides per pass whether
it runs, by the shader's own conditions (RAVU only for a picture drawn
more than 1.41 times its size in width and height, FSRCNNX more than
1.3 times); dvbridge_render_upscaled() says whether the last picture
was drawn with them.

A shader that this GPU cannot draw never costs a picture: when
libplacebo turns a hook off, or the render fails with the hooks, the
picture is drawn again without them, and they stay off until another
upscaler is set (dvbridge_render_upscaler_failed()). Measured on the
software bridge with a compute shader that the GLES backend cannot
create: the picture comes out byte for byte as without an upscaler.

A plain picture can now also be SDR (dvbridge_plain::sdr and
::sdr_primaries: the BT.709, BT.601 or BT.2020 matrix and the primaries
that Kodi's own shaders take for it, BT.1886), drawn as it is into
BT.709, so that an SDR video can be upscaled. Nothing maps it: a
mapping to a display or HDR over the tunnel refuses it, as an SDR
picture does not belong there.

Without an upscaler set, every picture is drawn as before: on the
software bridge, HDR10 drawn as it is, mapped to SDR and adapted to a
display give the same bytes as before this change.
